chapter(1,'INTRODUCTION','This chapter introduces AquaSense AI, the problem of fragmented groundwater and water-quality observations, the project objectives, scope and organisation of the report.')
p('1.1 Overview', 'Heading 2'); p('Groundwater monitoring involves measurements that must be interpreted together with time, station identity and location. Manual readings are often difficult to compare, while device readings without review can be mistaken for official evidence. AquaSense AI provides a single workflow for observation entry, location context, administrator approval, trend analysis and reporting.')
p('1.2 Problem Statement','Heading 2'); p('Existing small-scale monitoring workflows commonly separate sensor output, water-quality interpretation, groundwater trends and reporting. They may also overclaim what a few sensors can prove. The project addresses the need for an auditable platform that preserves raw readings, validates inputs, links them to a site, supports ESP32 ingestion and communicates the limits of sensor-based screening.')
p('1.3 Objectives','Heading 2');
for x in ['Collect groundwater-level readings from an ESP32 distance-sensor workflow.','Collect four water-quality parameters: calibrated pH, TDS in ppm, turbidity in NTU and temperature compensation data.','Attach measurement time and current site location to submitted observations.','Provide user accounts, protected administration and a review gate before analytics promotion.','Support map-based station selection, history, reports and transparent model/fallback outputs.','Avoid claiming potability or contaminant detection beyond the measured parameters.']: p(x, style='List Bullet')
p('1.4 Scope and Organisation','Heading 2'); p('The scope includes the web application, FastAPI backend, local SQLite fallback, ESP32 serial/HTTP bridge interface, map and location workflow, quality screening, groundwater analytics, authentication, review and reporting. Hardware calibration, laboratory certification, final institutional formatting, measured accuracy studies and production email credentials are outside the verified scope and are listed as completion gaps.')

chapter(2,'LITERATURE SURVEY','This chapter positions AquaSense against common water-quality indexing, IoT monitoring and groundwater forecasting approaches. Specific papers, authors and publication details are intentionally left for the authors to complete from their approved literature sources.')
