chapter(4,'ALGORITHMS AND MODEL DESIGN','This chapter records the implemented screening logic and the integration boundary for trained artifacts.')
p('4.1 Validation and Compensation','Heading 2'); p('Each parameter is represented by configuration containing its name, unit, precision, valid sensor range and screening limits. Temperature is stored with the observation and used as a compensation/context value for calibrated pH and any temperature-sensitive sensor conversion. The exact sensor calibration coefficients are [TO BE COMPLETED FROM THE HARDWARE CALIBRATION PROCEDURE].')
p('4.2 Screening Formula','Heading 2'); p('The current browser/backend screening calculates parameter sub-indices against configured limits and combines available contributions using documented weights. The exact standard, weights and calibration coefficients must be confirmed by the authors before the report claims a regulatory WQI method.')
p('4.3 Groundwater Models','Heading 2'); p('The application provides integration interfaces for trained forecasting artifacts and a clearly labelled fallback when weights are unavailable. Candidate algorithms may include Random Forest, XGBoost, LSTM and Temporal Fusion Transformer, but the final selected model, feature order, training data split, metrics and model version are [TO BE COMPLETED FROM THE ACTUAL TRAINING RUN].')
p('4.3.1 Current Quality-Model Contract','Heading 3'); p('The active artifact is a compatible Extra Trees classifier using pH, TDS, turbidity and temperature in that order. The feature vector, units, valid sensor ranges and class mapping are validated before inference. The model is trained on formula-derived screening labels from 300 compatible records; the reported metrics therefore measure agreement with the screening-label process, not independent laboratory truth.')
p('4.3.2 Label and Weight Interpretation','Heading 3'); p('The four-parameter profile uses equal 25% contributions. Turbidity has the highest observed feature importance in the current artifact, but this must not be interpreted as causal evidence. Independent laboratory-labelled evaluation remains required before claiming real-world accuracy.')
p('4.3.3 Acidic Readings','Heading 3'); p('The current quality_inference.py path rejects pH values below 6.5 instead of accepting and classifying them as abnormal or unsuitable. This is a validation/classification design issue: acidic readings are valid observations even when they fall outside the preferred screening range. The final implementation should preserve the reading, flag it, and return a bounded classification with an explanatory warning rather than failing inference solely because pH is low.')
p('4.4 Model Contract','Heading 2'); p('Inference validates feature names, order, units and ranges before prediction. A mismatch is rejected with a clear error. Model outputs are not presented as laboratory measurements or certainty about drinking-water safety.')

